; Platform-variant constants in a SCRIPT (no explicit `fn main`): the variant
; matching the host must win, and only that variant may contribute a value.
;
; Regression for the MIR script path. `nodeMatchesPlatform` (src/mir/platform.go)
; was consulted by the top-level registration loop but NOT by
; synthesizeMainForTopLevel (src/mir/hir2mir.go), so a platform-filtered variant
; was still inlined as a script local and its wrong-platform value overwrote the
; global that the matching variant had registered. On arm64 macOS the broken MIR
; printed 600 (the LAST variant, `win-amd64`).
;
; NOTE — the legacy backend is NOT the oracle here. Measured with a
; pre-deletion binary (git worktree add <tmp> 47b6cad): legacy prints 600 too,
; i.e. it never filtered platform variants in script mode at all (a lone
; `#{mac-amd64}` variant also just printed its value). The fix therefore makes
; MIR follow the DOCUMENTED meaning of the annotation (docs/docs/lang/syntax.md)
; rather than restoring legacy parity, and this file now deliberately DIVERGES
; from tests/golden/legacy-baseline.tsv. With an explicit `fn main` both
; backends already filtered identically.
;
; Every variant holds a DISTINCT value, so on whichever host this runs a wrong
; pick shows up as a changed stdout hash in the frozen golden baseline
; (scripts/mir_golden.sh + tests/golden/mir-baseline.tsv), which is now the
; regression oracle for this file. The same rules are pinned as Go unit tests in
; src/mir/platform_test.go. The std sources could not catch the original bug:
; std/fs.no's mac-amd64 and mac-arm64 constants happen to hold equal values
; (O-CREAT 512/512, O-TRUNC 1024/1024), which made the wrong pick invisible.

#{mac-arm64}
PV = 100

#{mac-amd64}
PV = 200

#{linux-arm64}
PV = 300

#{linux-amd64}
PV = 400

#{win-arm64}
PV = 500

#{win-amd64}
PV = 600

print(PV)
